<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moncef Bey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1881">1881</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad VII al-Munsif · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Moncef Bey (Muhammad VII al-Munsif) was bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> from June 1942 to May 1943. Sympathetic to the nationalists, he asserted his authority against the French resident-general and appointed a government without French approval. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, the Free French deposed him in 1943 on charges of collaboration with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, and he died in exile at Pau in 1948, remembered as a nationalist hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moncef-bey.jpg" alt="Moncef Bey" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moncef_bey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad VII al-Munsif was born at La Manouba on 4 March 1881, the son of Muhammad V an-Nasir, and was the penultimate ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husainid-dynasty">Husainid dynasty</a>. In April 1922 he supported the nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/destour">Destour</a> movement and persuaded his father, Naceur Bey, to receive its representatives. In October 1900 he married his second cousin Lalla Traki Beya, daughter of Muhammad IV al-Hadi, who bore his four children. He was invested as Bey al-Mahalla on 30 April 1942 and succeeded his first cousin once removed, Ahmed Bey, on 19 June 1942.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime gave him the Grand Cross of the Légion d'honneur, Moncef Bey sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a> a memorandum on 2 August 1942 reasserting Tunisian sovereignty. He asked for a consultative legislative council with a Tunisian majority, civil service posts for Tunisians, measures against poverty, compulsory schooling in Arabic and the nationalisation of key enterprises. At the Eid al-Fitr ceremonies of 12 October 1942 the Resident General, Admiral Jean-Pierre Esteva, told him that only the French were suited to positions of authority, and the Bey asked in vain for his recall.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops arrived on 16 November 1942, he proclaimed Tunisian neutrality while secretly assuring Roosevelt that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> would support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and refused an Italian offer to repudiate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>. On 1 January 1943 he named Mohamed Chenik prime minister. Known as the Protector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, he worked to keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> decrees against the Jewish community from being applied and refused to sign others, among them those imposing the yellow star and forced labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, the French colonial lobby around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> accused him of collaborating with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>. On 13 May 1943 General Alphonse Juin, the acting Resident General, demanded his abdication on Giraud's orders; he refused, was removed by Giraud's decree the next day and was flown out of the country. He formally abdicated at Laghouat in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> on 8 July, was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenes">Ténès</a> and, on 17 October 1945, to Pau, where he died. He was buried with full honours in the Jellaz Cemetery in Tunis, and the Place Moncef-Bey in La Marsa was named after him on 1 September 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Moncef Bey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Moncef Bey</b> was deposed by the French in 1943.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tunisia">French protectorate of Tunisia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tunis, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Pau, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.8,10.18&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Tunis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4805 837 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Moncef Bey"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 1107h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 1107h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.8,10.18&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
